<p class="kicker">HFT Practice · Lesson 0040</p>
<h1>Streamlit's Execution Model: Whole-Script Reruns and Where State Lives</h1>
<div class="why"><strong>Why this matters:</strong> the platform now has a real
Streamlit app (<code>streamlit_app/dashboard.py</code>, PR #75). Streamlit's core
trick — rerun the ENTIRE script on every interaction — is what makes it feel
effortless, and it is also the thing that silently breaks apps whose authors
don't know where state is allowed to live.</div>

<h2 id="s1">1 · The model</h2>
<p>Click a button, type in a box, switch a tab: Streamlit throws away the frame
and executes your script again from line 1. Widgets aren't event handlers —
they're <em>functions that return their current value during this rerun</em>.
<code>st.button</code> returns <code>True</code> exactly once (the rerun caused by the
click), then <code>False</code> on every later rerun. There is no callback soup and
no DOM to reconcile — but it means module-level variables reset on every
interaction, so anything that must SURVIVE a rerun needs an explicit home:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>State</th><th>Correct home</th><th>In our console</th></tr>
<tr><td>Widget values</td><td>The widgets themselves (keyed by position/label)</td><td>Sidebar paths, tickers, interval</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accumulated data across interactions</td><td><code>st.session_state</code></td><td><code>tape_seen</code> (signal dedupe), <code>tape_lines</code> (bounded scrollback)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Module globals</td><td><strong>Nowhere</strong> — reset every rerun</td><td>Deliberately none</td></tr>
</table>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># dashboard.py — why the tape survives the rerun the button causes:</span>
seen = st.session_state.setdefault("tape_seen", {})
lines = st.session_state.setdefault("tape_lines", [])
if refresh:                      <span class="cm"># True only on the click-rerun</span>
    lines.append(...)            <span class="cm"># mutates the session_state object</span>
    st.session_state["tape_lines"] = lines[-200:]  <span class="cm"># bounded scrollback</span></code></pre>

<h2 id="s2">2 · The two guards this repo added</h2>
<p><strong>Import-safety guard.</strong> <code>streamlit run</code> executes the file
top-to-bottom, so apps put their build at module level — but our suite must be
able to import the module in an environment with NO streamlit. The fix pins the
launch to a real script-run context:</p>
<pre><code>if __name__ == "__main__" or _in_streamlit_run():
    main()
<span class="cm"># _in_streamlit_run(): streamlit.runtime.scriptrunner.get_script_run_ctx()
# is not None — true under `streamlit run` AND under AppTest, false for
# plain imports. Pinned by test_plain_import_never_launches_the_app.</span></code></pre>
<p><strong>Logic/presentation split.</strong> Reruns make UI code awkward to unit
test — so everything testable lives in <code>streamlit_app/data.py</code> (pure, no
streamlit import, read-only <code>mode=ro</code> SQLite), and the UI file is thin
enough that <code>streamlit.testing.v1.AppTest</code> can exercise it whole: run the
script, assert on <code>at.metric</code>/<code>at.dataframe</code>/<code>at.exception</code>.</p>

<h2 id="s3">3 · Often confused</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Pair</th><th>Distinction</th></tr>
<tr><td>Rerun model vs callback model (Tkinter/FastAPI)</td><td>gui_app.py wires callbacks to long-lived widgets; webapp routes handle one request each. Streamlit re-executes everything and RECONSTRUCTS widgets — state lives in session_state, not in the widget tree or module.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>st.session_state</code> vs <code>@st.cache_data</code></td><td>session_state is per-browser-tab mutable memory; cache_data memoizes function results across reruns (and users) keyed by arguments. The tape's dedupe dict must NOT be cached — it's stateful, not a pure function result.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The lab web theme vs this console</td><td>The webapp's "lab" theme is FastAPI wearing Streamlit's CSS; this is actual Streamlit — reruns, session_state, AppTest and all. Two different programs that happen to share an aesthetic.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="s4">4 · Practice</h2>
